spurious correlation ≠ every noncausal correlation
spurious correlation ≠ shortcut learning
shortcut learning ≠ theorem explaining all generalization failure
</pre>

== Machine-learning scope audit ==

[[Machine learning]] is not defined as all AI.

It includes multiple learning paradigms without reducing the field to supervised deep learning.

It links to [[Learning]] while preserving the computational/biological distinction.

== Generalization audit ==

[[Generalization (machine learning)]] is defined relative to new examples/conditions beyond the fitted examples.

The article distinguishes ordinary iid-style generalization from out-of-distribution robustness.

A low training/test gap is not treated as sufficient evidence of good performance because both errors can be high.

Memorization and generalization are allowed to coexist.

== Overfitting / underfitting audit ==

[[Overfitting]] is a relationship among finite data, learning procedure, and target performance rather than a synonym for a large model.

The page explicitly rejects:

<pre>
large model → necessarily overfit
interpolation → necessarily overfit
memorization → necessarily no generalization
</pre>

[[Underfitting]] is not defined only by insufficient parameter count.

Representation, optimization, excessive regularization, feature quality and objective mismatch remain possible causes.

== Bias–variance audit ==

The classical bias–variance decomposition is preserved under its statistical assumptions.

The wave does not canonize the old single-U-curve story as a universal law.

Belkin et al. (2019) provides the modern correction: interpolation-era systems can exhibit double-descent behavior.

The Technical page separates the squared-error decomposition from empirical test-error-versus-capacity curves.

== Dataset-role audit ==

[[Training set]], [[Validation set]], and [[Test set]] are defined by function, not fixed proportions.

The pages preserve:

<pre>
training → direct fitting
validation → development/model selection
test → independent post-selection evaluation
</pre>

Cross-validation can rotate roles.

Repeated test consultation makes the test data part of an adaptive development loop even if the file is still named “test”.

== Distribution-shift audit ==

[[Distribution shift]] is defined as:

<pre>
P_train(X,Y) ≠ P_target(X,Y)
</pre>

in the general case.

Covariate shift is correctly treated as a special case with stable <code>P(Y|X)</code>, not as the definition of all shift.

The page separates shift from ordinary overfitting.

== Leakage audit ==

[[Data leakage (machine learning)]] covers:

* target leakage;
* temporal/future leakage;
* preprocessing leakage;
* duplicate/group overlap;
* train–test contamination;
* indirect target information.

It is explicitly distinct from security data exfiltration.

Learned preprocessing is treated as part of the fitting pipeline.

== Benchmark-contamination audit ==

[[Benchmark contamination]] is treated as an evaluation-specific contamination problem and not as an LLM-only concept.

The page includes direct, near-duplicate/derivative, fine-tuning, synthetic-data and benchmark-targeted adaptation channels.

The audit preserves a current empirical nuance: contamination can inflate scores, but the effect size depends on exposure, similarity, optimization and forgetting. Contamination is not asserted to make every affected score completely meaningless.

[[Benchmark contamination]] is also separated from benchmark saturation, construct validity, annotation quality and metric gaming.

== Model-selection-bias audit ==

[[Model selection bias]] follows the Cawley & Talbot distinction:

<pre>
individual validation estimate can be unbiased
+
selection over noisy estimates
→ optimistic selected estimate
</pre>

It is not merged into [[Selection bias]], which concerns sample/observation selection.

Nested cross-validation is represented as one mechanism for separating model-selection and performance-estimation roles.

== Spurious-correlation audit ==

[[Spurious correlation]] remains broader than machine learning and links to [[Correlation]], [[Causality]], and [[Selection bias]].

The article avoids the inaccurate rule:

<pre>
noncausal correlation = spurious correlation
</pre>

A noncausal predictor can remain useful under a stable specified deployment distribution.

The term “spurious” is instead tied to unstable/misleading task relevance under the intended environment.

== Shortcut-learning audit ==

[[Shortcut learning]] is assigned '''Supported''', not Established, because it is a productive research framing rather than a universal law of learning systems.

The pages preserve:

<pre>
spurious correlation = relation/data property
shortcut learning = learner's use of an easy decision rule
</pre>

They also allow models to learn robust and shortcut features simultaneously.

Geirhos et al. (2020) anchors the concept; later work is used to show the active state of shortcut-mitigation research rather than to overstate consensus.

== Public concept versus local/project application ==

The wave preserves:

<pre>
machine learning       ≠ BITwiki agent behavior
generalization         ≠ “works on our prompts”
overfitting            ≠ “model too big”
training set            ≠ local corpus by definition
validation set          ≠ staging environment
test set                ≠ CI test suite
distribution shift      ≠ project version drift
data leakage            ≠ infrastructure secret leak
benchmark contamination ≠ benchmark disagreement
model selection bias    ≠ governance selection
shortcut learning       ≠ tool-use shortcut
</pre>

No local agent architecture is used to define a public concept.

